IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-logic`
IMPORT `ucta-schedules`

-- Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation.
-- LAYER 1, MODULE 6: PART 1, INTRODUCTORY, section 1 (Scope of this Part).
-- The reach aggregator at the end of this module is the one place where sections 2 to 7 are told whether
-- an exception takes them out of the case (s 1(2) and (3), First Schedule, Part 3).
--
-- READING A RESULT (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
--   * JUST FALSE : the text says no in its own words;
--   * JUST TRUE  : the text says yes in its own words;
--   * NOTHING    : not covered.
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.

§ `Section 1 - scope of this Part`

-- src:86 | 1.—(1) For the purposes of this Part, “negligence” means the
-- src:88 | breach —
-- src:90 | (a) of any obligation, arising from the express or implied terms
-- src:91 | of a contract, to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable
-- src:92 | skill in the performance of the contract; or
-- src:94 | (b) of any common law duty to take reasonable care or
-- src:95 | exercise reasonable skill (but not any stricter duty).
@ref s 1(1) (src:86-95)
GIVEN `the breach is of an obligation, arising from the express or implied terms of a contract, to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill in the performance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the breach is of a common law duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill, and not of any stricter duty` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1(1) the breach is negligence` MEANS
    IF `the breach is of an obligation, arising from the express or implied terms of a contract, to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill in the performance of the contract`
        OR `the breach is of a common law duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill, and not of any stricter duty`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "negligence means the breach (a) of any obligation ... to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill in the performance of the contract; or (b) of any common law duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill (but not any stricter duty)"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "negligence means ..." -- a definition that "means"; a breach that is neither (a) nor (b) is not negligence for the purposes of this Part
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:102 | (3) In the case of both contract and tort, sections 2 to 7 apply
-- src:103 | (except where the contrary is stated in section 6(4)) only to business
-- src:104 | liability, that is liability for breach of obligations or duties arising
-- src:105 | from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business
-- src:106 | (whether his own business or another’s), and references to liability
-- src:107 | are to be read accordingly.
@ref s 1(3), "business liability" (src:102-107)
GIVEN `the liability is for breach of obligations or duties arising from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether the person's own business or another's)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1(3) the liability is business liability` MEANS
    IF `the liability is for breach of obligations or duties arising from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether the person's own business or another's)`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "business liability, that is liability for breach of obligations or duties arising from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether his own business or another's)"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "business liability, that is liability for ..." -- a definition by "that is"; any other liability is not business liability
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:109 | (4) In relation to any breach of duty or obligation, it is immaterial
-- src:110 | for any purpose of this Part whether the breach was inadvertent or
-- src:111 | intentional, or whether liability for it arises directly or vicariously.
@ref s 1(4) (src:109-111)
GIVEN `the point asked about is whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or whether liability for it arises directly or vicariously` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1(4) it makes a difference for this Part` MEANS
    IF `the point asked about is whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or whether liability for it arises directly or vicariously`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "it is immaterial for any purpose of this Part whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or whether liability for it arises directly or vicariously"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (4) speaks only of those two distinctions
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:97 | (2) This Part is subject to Part 3; and in relation to contracts, the
-- src:98 | operation of sections 2 to 4 and 7 is subject to the exceptions made by
-- src:99 | the First Schedule.
-- src:102 | (3) In the case of both contract and tort, sections 2 to 7 apply
-- src:103 | (except where the contrary is stated in section 6(4)) only to business
-- src:104 | liability, that is liability for breach of obligations or duties arising
-- src:105 | from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business
-- src:106 | (whether his own business or another’s), and references to liability
-- src:107 | are to be read accordingly.
-- src:543 | 1. Sections 2 to 4 do not extend to —
-- src:545 | (a) any contract of insurance (including a contract to pay an annuity on
-- src:546 | human life);
-- src:548 | (b) any contract so far as it relates to the creation or transfer of an interest
-- src:549 | in land, or to the termination of such an interest, whether by extinction,
-- src:550 | merger, surrender, forfeiture or otherwise;
-- src:552 | (c) any contract so far as it relates to the creation or transfer of a right or
-- src:553 | interest in any patent, trade mark, copyright, registered design,
-- src:554 | technical or commercial information or other intellectual property,
-- src:555 | or relates to the termination of any such right or interest;
-- src:556 | 2020 Ed. Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 13
-- src:558 | FIRST SCHEDULE — continued
-- src:560 | (d) any contract so far as it relates —
-- src:562 | (i) to the formation or dissolution of a company (which means any
-- src:563 | body corporate or unincorporated association and includes a
-- src:564 | partnership); or
-- src:566 | (ii) to its constitution or the rights or obligations of its corporators
-- src:567 | or members; and
-- src:569 | (e) any contract so far as it relates to the creation or transfer of securities or
-- src:570 | of any right or interest in securities.
-- src:572 | 2. Section 2(1) extends to —
-- src:574 | (a) any contract of marine salvage or towage;
-- src:576 | (b) any charterparty of a ship or hovercraft; and
-- src:578 | (c) any contract for the carriage of goods by ship or hovercraft,
-- src:580 | but subject to this, sections 2 to 4 and 7 do not extend to any such contract except
-- src:581 | in favour of a person dealing as consumer.
-- src:583 | 3. Where goods are carried by ship or hovercraft in pursuance of a contract
-- src:584 | which either —
-- src:586 | (a) specifies that as the means of carriage over part of the journey to be
-- src:587 | covered; or
-- src:589 | (b) makes no provision as to the means of carriage and does not exclude
-- src:590 | that means,
-- src:592 | then sections 2(2), 3 and 4 do not, except in favour of a person dealing as
-- src:593 | consumer, extend to the contract as it operates for and in relation to the carriage of
-- src:594 | the goods by that means.
-- src:596 | 4. Subsections (1) and (2) of section 2 do not extend to a contract of
-- src:597 | employment, except in favour of the employee.
@ref s 1(2) and (3), with the First Schedule (src:97-99)
@ref s 1(3) (src:102-107)
@ref First Schedule, paragraphs 1 to 4 (src:543-597)
GIVEN section IS A `Section of Part 1`
      scope IS A `Scope facts`
      `Part 3 takes it` IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A `Reach of a section`
`s 1(2) and (3) whether an exception takes the section out of the case` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `section 1(3) takes it out` OR (`the Schedule takes it out` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`Part 3 takes it` EQUALS `An exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): s 1(3) "sections 2 to 7 apply ... only to business liability"; or the First Schedule, "do not extend to"; or a Part 3 provision
        THEN `An exception takes the section out of the case`
        IF (`the Schedule takes it out` EQUALS NOTHING) OR (`Part 3 takes it` EQUALS `Whether an exception takes the section out of the case cannot be told`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether the person protected deals as consumer is not known and the Schedule answers differently on either answer, or Part 3 turns on something outside the text (the Minister's order, section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909)
        THEN `Whether an exception takes the section out of the case cannot be told`
        OTHERWISE `No exception takes the section out of the case`
    WHERE
        -- s 1(3): "(except where the contrary is stated in section 6(4))": section 6 reaches also any contract of sale of goods or hire-purchase agreement.
        `section 1(3) takes it out` MEANS
                (NOT scope's `the liability is business liability`)
            AND (NOT (section EQUALS `Section 6` AND (   scope's `kind of contract` EQUALS `A contract of sale of goods`
                                                      OR scope's `kind of contract` EQUALS `A hire-purchase agreement`)))
        -- First Schedule, read for the consumer status of the person protected.
        `the Schedule takes it out` MEANS
            CONSIDER scope's `the person protected deals as consumer`
            WHEN JUST x THEN JUST (`Schedule out when consumer is` x)
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                IF `Schedule out when consumer is` TRUE EQUALS `Schedule out when consumer is` FALSE
                THEN JUST (`Schedule out when consumer is` TRUE)
                ELSE NOTHING
        `Schedule out when consumer is` c MEANS
            CONSIDER section
            -- para 1 names ss 2 to 4; para 2 gives s 2(1) its own extension; para 4 names s 2(1) and (2).
            WHEN `Section 2(1)` THEN `para 1 no` OR `para 4 no`
            WHEN `Section 2(2)` THEN `para 1 no` OR `para 2 no` c OR `para 3 no` c OR `para 4 no`
            WHEN `Section 3` THEN `para 1 no` OR `para 2 no` c OR `para 3 no` c
            WHEN `Section 4` THEN `para 1 no` OR `para 2 no` c OR `para 3 no` c
            -- para 2 names ss 2 to 4 and 7: s 7 is reached by para 2 only.
            WHEN `Section 7` THEN `para 2 no` c
            WHEN `Section 5` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `Section 6` THEN FALSE
        `para 1 no` MEANS `is a no` (`sch 1 para 1 sections 2 to 4 extend to the contract` (scope's `what the contract relates to`))
        `para 2 no` c MEANS `is a no` (`sch 1 para 2 sections 2 to 4 and 7, subject to section 2(1), extend to the contract` (scope's `what the contract relates to`) c)
        `para 3 no` c MEANS `is a no` (`sch 1 para 3 sections 2(2), 3 and 4 extend to the contract as it operates for the carriage of the goods by ship or hovercraft` scope c)
        `para 4 no` MEANS `is a no` (`sch 1 para 4 section 2(1) and (2) extend to the contract` (scope's `what the contract relates to`) (scope's `the person protected is the employee`))
